  "notes": "West leads the ♦Q against 6♣. South has a possible loser in spades and another in hearts. It looks like the contract will succeed if either finesse is right or if hearts break 3-3. In the latter case, one of dummy's spades can be discarded on the fourth round of hearts. South's ♠Q can then be ruffed in the dummy.\n\nSouth can further improve his chances, making the contract when West has a doubleton ♥K, by playing for an elimination. After winning the opening diamond lead, South should trump a diamond in the closed hand.\n\nThe dummy is reached in clubs and North's last diamond is ruffed. South next cashes the ♥A and crosses back to the dummy in clubs, drawing the last trump in the process.\n\nA heart is played from the dummy and, when East follows low, South plays the ♥J or ♥Q. If West started with the doubleton ♥K, he will be endplayed, forced to either lead spades into South's tenace or concede a ruff and discard.\n\nSouth loses none of his original chances by playing this way. If East has the ♥K, South can take 3 heart tricks by leading that suit twice from the dummy. A heart winner will be established to discard a spade from dummy.",
